<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dali Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">937 CE – 1253 CE · capital Yangjumie (Dali)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Buddhist kingdom of the Duan family in Yunnan, which ruled the south-west for over three centuries until the Mongol conquest of 1253.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-siping">Duan Siping</a>, a commander of Bai descent from the Tonghai region, overthrew <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/da-yining">Da Yining</a> with the help of tribal allies from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and founded the Dali Kingdom, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangjumie">Yangjumie</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-erhai">Lake Erhai</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dali was a devoutly Buddhist state; esoteric Acharya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was strong, and many kings abdicated to become monks. From the late 11th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> family held hereditary office as chief ministers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-shengtai">Gao Shengtai</a> briefly took the throne in 1094 before the Duan were restored in 1096. Dali sold horses to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> and dominated routes to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Burma</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1253 a Mongol army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai</a> took the capital; the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-xingzhi">Duan Xingzhi</a>, fled but was captured in 1254 and submitted. The Duan family continued as hereditary governors under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in 1382.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dali-kingdom.jpg" alt="Dali Kingdom" width="320" height="407"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dali_Kingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>937 CE – 1253 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yangjumie (Dali)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duan Siping, Duan Zhengyan, Duan Xingzhi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bai, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dali, Kingdom of Dali</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1095&amp;focus=dali-kingdom" aria-label="Dali Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7334 1234 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dali Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7654 1490L7901 1174M7654 1490L7559 780"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7901 1174h0M7559 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7654 1490h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7654 1490h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1095&amp;focus=dali-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
